on escalated  taking over this plot that would  eventually become the city's first  official community garden we had  problems with people coming  into the space and trying to deal drugs  one guy  tried to rip off a branch from a tree we  chased him down the subway with  pitchforks  he never came back again he probably  didn't expect to get chased by  pitchforks in manhattan  probably not but it worked it kept them  away and as the plants grew  so did the concept all over the city  people began  tilling their blighted blocks you want  to get as much of the roof as possible  today the gardens are as diverse as the  neighborhoods they represent  every garden is different it's a  reflection of the neighborhood and their  needs and wants and wishes  for for they want to make new york  better  carlos martinez is the deputy director  of new york city's green thumb program  which helps provide seeds and support  for the otherwise self-funded gardens  if these weren't maintained by  volunteers would the city be able to  keep up gardens like this  it would be very difficult for the city  to maintain like so many gardens so  we really value all the bloodstream and  tears that all the our gardeners  put into this originally from columbia  martinez says the gardens are popular  with new new yorkers  looking to put down some roots many of  our immigrant gardeners  join a community garden because they  want to reflect of home  at tranquility farms in bedsky brooklyn  it's the forest cynthia which is the  plant of new york city inna mcpherson  brings a bit of her jamaican heritage to  this inner city soil  i always say my mom raised us in an  urban setting with rural sensibilities  she teaches local kids where the food  they see packaged and processed  actually comes from this is arugula and  shows them eggs from her brood of  chickens  which she insisted we hold how do i put  the chicken down  i don't want to stick her like this and  all right she says the gardens bring  value beyond plants and produce  i mean look what's going on in here  there's birds in here there are trees  nature abounds in here and you if you  have all these green spaces  you know how are you going to live  living without them is a constant fear  while many gardens are protected the  threat of development is  always there still the once dirt cover  disrupters have been embraced by their  communities  giving loggins more time to tend to his  thoughts  it's so nice to come here and just enjoy  yourself and realize  you know how green the city can be and  peaceful well some of the time  for cbs this morning saturday christina  raffini new york  you would think that people would really  love to have  i mean developers even to have a  community garden  in their neighborhood right that they  wouldn't fight it too much i think  they're thinking  money sometimes for developers i agree  with you it's beautiful the balance  yeah there's a balance to be found  though between the development and and  keeping some of these and people that  don't live in in the city like new york  don't realize  it is a nice little escape even if you  hear that siren just sitting in that  environment well back in the day  everybody had a backyard garden  you know so now we're getting back to it

